WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 232: Portugal: Prince Henry the Navigator, Vasco da Gama, and the Sea Route to India

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.080
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:17.200
[Rebecca] Today, we stand on the windswept Atlantic cliffs of Sagars at the southwestern tip of the European continent to follow the brave Portuguese mariners who charted the unknown oceans.

3
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:25.360
[Rebecca] Prince Henry the Navigator, Vasco da Gama, and the dawn of the Age of Discovery.

4
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:39.840
[George] At the beginning of the 15th century, Portugal was a small, poor kingdom perched on the edge of the known European world, hemmed in by the powerful kingdom of Castile to the east and the terrifying expanse of the uncharted Atlantic Ocean to the west.

5
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:52.240
[George] Europeans believed that beyond Cape Bajador on the western coast of Africa, the ocean boiled with sulfurous heat, sea monsters swallowed entire ships, and the sun burned human skin black.

6
00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:02.000
[George] Into this era of medieval superstition stepped Prince Henry of Portugal, third son of King John I, known to history as Prince Henry the Navigator.

7
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:18.240
[George] Setting up his court on the desolate, rocky promontory of Sagars, where the cliffs plunged into the churning Atlantic breakers, Henry assembled an extraordinary intellectual guild of Jewish and Arab cartographers, Christian navigators, astronomers, and instrument makers.

8
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:36.720
[George] Henry recognized that mastering the ocean required transforming navigation from folklore into an exact mathematical science, systematically collecting navigational charts, improving the astrolabe and quadrant, and financing annual exploratory expeditions down the western coast of Africa.

9
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:51.280
[George] Prince Henry's court at Sagars became a vibrant intellectual sanctuary where scholars translated ancient Arabic astronomical treatises into Latin and compiled tables of solar declination that enabled mariners to determine their latitude at sea.

10
00:01:51.280 --> 00:02:04.440
[George] Prince Henry's navigational observatory at Sagars collected intelligence from traveling North African merchants, analyzing trade caravan routes across the Sahara Desert in search of the legendary gold mines of Timbuktu.

11
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:14.120
[George] To conquer the Atlantic, Henry's shipwrights in the shipyards of Lagos developed a revolutionary vessel that changed the course of human history, the Caravel.

12
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:29.720
[George] Unlike the heavy, square-rigged merchant ships of the Mediterranean, which were helpless when the wind blew against them, the Caravel was a lightweight, shallow draft ship of 50 to 70 tons, equipped with triangular Latin sails borrowed from Arab Dhows.

13
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:38.840
[George] This Latine rigging allowed Portuguese captains to perform the miracle of tacking, sailing into the wind in a zigzag pattern at 45 degree angles.

14
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:46.040
[George] If a caravel encountered a shallow river or an uncharted reef, its light draft prevented catastrophic grounding.

15
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:51.080
[George] If it sailed into uncharted wind patterns, it could always tack its way back home.

16
00:02:51.080 --> 00:03:06.600
[George] With the caravel, the ocean ceased to be an impassable barrier and became an open highway of discovery, enabling Portuguese explorers to venture thousands of miles into the Atlantic, discovering and colonizing the archipelagos of Madeira and the Azores.

17
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:17.880
[George] The Portuguese Latin Caravel was so maneuverable that Arab and Venetian sailors who encountered them along the African coast marveled at how effortlessly they could dance against prevailing trade winds.

18
00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:31.000
[George] The caravel's lightweight wooden hull was constructed using overlapping pine and oak planks sealed with pitch and tar, allowing the vessel to bounce off rocky sandbanks without suffering catastrophic structural failure.

19
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:46.240
[George] For twelve long years, Prince Henry dispatched expedition after expedition to round Cape Bajador in western Sahara, and each year his terrified captains turned back, paralyzed by ancient sailors' myths of boiling waters and boiling sea serpents.

20
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:55.920
[George] Finally, in 1434, a fearless squire named Gil Eanes made a solemn vow to his prince that he would pass the dreaded Cape or die trying.

21
00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:05.440
[George] Instead of hugging the hazardous reef-strewn coast, Eanes sailed far out to sea into the deep Atlantic, looping around the Cape before turning back toward the coast.

22
00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:16.400
[George] When he landed south of Bajador, he found no monsters and no boiling seas, but gentle rolling waves and sandy dunes blooming with delicate yellow wild flowers known as St.

23
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.840
[George] Mary's Roses.

24
00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:26.960
[George] Eanes dug up a bouquet of the flowers, planted them in a wooden barrel of sand, and sailed triumphantly back to Lisbon to present them to Prince Henry.

25
00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.960
[George] The psychological terror of the unknown ocean was shattered forever.

26
00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.560
[George] The gateway to the southern hemisphere was flung wide open.

27
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:47.360
[George] When Gil Eames returned with the humble wild roses of Bajador, Prince Henry wept with joy, realizing that the psychological phantom that had paralyzed European navigation for centuries had vanished forever.

28
00:04:47.360 --> 00:05:02.960
[George] Gil Eans's courageous breakthrough past Cape Bajador shattered centuries of maritime superstition, opening a glorious half-century of Portuguese oceanic exploration that transformed Europe from an isolated subcontinent into a global maritime empire.

29
00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:20.560
[George] Over the next fifty years, Portuguese mariners pushed steadily down the African continent, establishing trading posts along the Gold Coast and crossing the equator into the southern heavens, where European sailors gazed upon the unfamiliar constellation of the Southern Cross for the first time.

30
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.760
[George] In August 1487, King John I.

31
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:34.760
[George] I commissioned veteran navigator Bartolomu Dayas with the supreme objective to find the southern terminus of Africa and discover whether an oceanic passage existed into the Indian Ocean.

32
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:45.960
[George] Sailing down the southwest coast of Africa, Dais's two caravels were seized by a terrifying, 13-day gale that drove them hundreds of miles south into freezing Antarctic waters.

33
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:51.880
[George] When the storm abated, Dais steered east to find the coast, but found only open water.

34
00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:58.440
[George] Turning north, he sighted land at present-day Mossel Bay on February 3, 1488.

35
00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.400
[George] He had rounded the tip of Africa without knowing it.

36
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:06.760
[George] Dais named the storm-tossed headland Cabo das Tormentas, the Cape of Storms.

37
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:08.200
[George] But King John I.

38
00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:20.040
[George] recognizing that the sea route to the legendary spice markets of India was finally within reach, rechristened it with an immortal name, Cabo de Boa Esperanza, the Cape of Good Hope.

39
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:33.640
[George] Daez's men faced ferocious subantarctic squalls that ripped their canvas sails and froze their hemp ropes, yet the captain's iron discipline kept his terrified crew from mutinying as they ventured into uncharted southern latitudes.

40
00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:42.440
[George] At Mossel Bay, Daez erected a limestone stone pillar topped with an iron cross, a padro, claiming the coastline for King John I.

41
00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:04.280
[George] Departing the river Tagas at Belame aboard his flagship, the Sao Gabriel, de Gama performed a breathtaking piece of navigational audacity.

42
00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:17.200
[George] Instead of battling adverse currents along the African coast, he swung far out into the open Atlantic in a colossal westward arc, sailing within a few hundred miles of Brazil to catch prevailing westerlies.

43
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:24.960
[George] Spending 93 continuous days out of sight of land, the longest open ocean voyage in human history up to that time.

44
00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:40.240
[George] Rounding the Cape of Good Hope, de Gama navigated the treacherous Mozambique Channel, surviving skirmishes with hostile sultanates, until he hired an expert Arab Gujarati navigator at Malindi who guided the Portuguese fleet across the Arabian Sea.

45
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:51.520
[George] On May 20, 1498, after ten months and twelve thousand miles at sea, de Gama's fleet dropped anchor off the lush, palm fringed coast of Calicut, India.

46
00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:58.480
[George] A Portuguese sailor was rowed ashore and was asked by two Tunisian merchants speaking Spanish what he had come seeking.

47
00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.640
[George] He replied with immortal simplicity Christians and spices.

48
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:15.680
[George] When da Gama's fleet reached the Swahili coast of East Africa, they were astonished to find wealthy stonebuilt sultanates like Kilwa and Mombasa trading in Chinese porcelain, Persian carpets, and Indian silk.

49
00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:27.920
[George] Vasco da Gama carried letters of friendship and royal gifts from King Manuel I to the Hindu Zamoran of Calicut, seeking to secure exclusive commercial treaties for the purchase of Malabar black pepper.

50
00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:39.600
[George] De Gama's return to Lisbon in 1499, carrying chests of precious black pepper, cinnamon, ginger and cloves, touched off a geopolitical and economic revolution.

51
00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:55.440
[George] For centuries, the lucrative spice trade had been a monopoly of Venetian and Arab merchants, who moved spices across overland silk road caravans and Red Sea Dows, marking up prices by 1,000% before they reached European dining tables.

52
00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:02.200
[George] Portugal now possessed a direct oceanic bypass that could deliver spices around Africa at a fraction of the cost.

53
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:25.080
[George] Under the visionary, ruthless generalship of Afonso de Albuquerque, the Portuguese constructed a maritime empire of naval choke points rather than territorial conquests, capturing Goa in India as the imperial viceregal capital, the strategic Strait of Hormuz at the mouth of the Persian Gulf, and the wealthy trading emporium of Malacca in Southeast Asia.

54
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:37.080
[George] Tiny Portugal, with a population barely exceeding one million people, became the supreme naval master of the Indian Ocean, dominating global spice commerce from Lisbon to Macau and Japan.

55
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:41.480
[George] Albuquerque's naval strategy was anchored in maritime choking points.

56
00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:51.560
[George] By erecting massive stone fortresses equipped with heavy bronze bombards at Ormas and Malacca, he controlled every entrance into the Indian Ocean with minimal ground forces.

57
00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:58.520
[George] Albuquerque's strategic brilliance lay in his understanding that sea power does not require territorial conquest.

58
00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:05.240
[George] By fortifying three key straits, he turned the entire Indian Ocean into a closed Portuguese trading lake.

59
00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:13.560
[George] The unimaginable wealth generated by the spice monopoly transformed Lisbon into the most opulent, cosmopolitan capital in Europe.

60
00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:20.360
[George] On the riverfront at Belame, where Vasco de Gama had spent his final night in prayer before departing for India.

61
00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:38.200
[George] King Manuel I commissioned the breathtaking Geronimos Monastery and the iconic Belame Tower, designed in the exuberant Manuelan architectural style, its limestone columns and vaults intricately carved with nautical ropes, anchors, armillary spheres, coral reefs, and sea monsters.

62
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:51.120
[George] When poet Luis de Camos published Os Lusiadas in 1572, he celebrated the Portuguese navigators as titans who sailed through seas never before navigated to unite the continents of the earth.

63
00:10:51.360 --> 00:11:06.000
[George] Though Portugal's commercial monopoly would eventually yield to Dutch and English rivals, its pioneering mariners had fundamentally rewritten the geography of the planet, transforming isolated regional civilizations into an interconnected global world.

64
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:20.320
[George] The tombs of Vasco da Gama and poet Luis de Camos lie opposite each other inside the Geronimos monastery, their carved limestone effigies resting upon marble sarcophagi as eternal guardians of Portugal's oceanic glory.

65
00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:34.880
[George] The limestone sculptures of the Belame Tower, rising directly from the waters of the Tagus, were designed with Moorish watchtowers, royal coats of arms, and carved stone rhinoceros heads celebrating the wonders of the new world.

66
00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.720
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

67
00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:43.840
[Rebecca] Next week, our Portuguese chronicle continues with episode 233.

68
00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:52.000
[Rebecca] Portugal Part 2 The Great Lisbon Earthquake of 1755, the Marx de Pombal, and the city reborn from the ashes.

69
00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.560
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

